{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nzscoder has a deck of $$$n+m$$$ custom-made cards, which consists of $$$n$$$ cards labelled from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$ and $$$m$$$ jokers. Since zscoder is lonely, he wants to play a game with himself using those cards. Initially, the deck is shuffled uniformly randomly and placed on the table. zscoder has a set $$$S$$$ which is initially empty. Every second, zscoder draws the top card from the deck.   If the card has a number $$$x$$$ written on it, zscoder removes the card and adds $$$x$$$ to the set $$$S$$$.  If the card drawn is a joker, zscoder places all the cards back into the deck and reshuffles (uniformly randomly) the $$$n+m$$$ cards to form a new deck (hence the new deck now contains all cards from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$ and the $$$m$$$ jokers). Then, if $$$S$$$ currently contains all the elements from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$, the game ends. Shuffling the deck doesn't take time at all. What is the expected number of seconds before the game ends? We can show that the answer can be written in the form $$$\\frac{P}{Q}$$$ where $$$P, Q$$$ are relatively prime integers and $$$Q \\neq 0 \\bmod 998244353$$$. Output the value of $$$(P \\cdot Q^{-1})$$$ modulo $$$998244353$$$.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new Main().start();\n    }\n\n    private void start() {\n        try (BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n             PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(System.out)) {\n            \n            String[] line = in.readLine().trim().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n            int n = Integer.parseInt(line[0]);\n            int m = Integer.parseInt(line[1]);\n            \n            Mod998 mod = new Mod998();\n            \n            long phaseCount = 1;\n            long product = 1;\n            for (int i = 0; product != 0; i++) {\n                long num = n - i;\n                long den = n + m - i;\n                product = mod.mult(product, mod.mult(num, mod.inv(den)));\n                phaseCount = mod.add(phaseCount, product);\n            }\n            \n            long finalAnswer = 0;\n            for (int k = 1; k <= n; k++) {\n                long sign = mod.paritySign(k + 1);\n                long numerator = mod.ncr(m + k - 1, k);\n                long denominator = mod.ncr(m + k, k);\n                long prNoSpecial = mod.div(numerator, denominator);\n                long invTerm = mod.inv(mod.subtract(1, prNoSpecial));\n                long expected = mod.mult(invTerm, phaseCount);\n                long comb = mod.ncr(n, k);\n                long term = mod.mult(comb, expected);\n                finalAnswer = mod.add(finalAnswer, mod.mult(sign, term));\n            }\n            \n            out.println(finalAnswer);\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            throw new RuntimeException(e);\n        }\n    }\n\n    static class Mod998 extends Modulus<Mod998> {\n        @Override\n        public long modulus() {\n            return 998_244_353L;\n        }\n    }\n\n    static abstract class Modulus<M extends Modulus<M>> {\n        private final List<Long> factorial = new ArrayList<>();\n        private final List<Long> invFactorial = new ArrayList<>();\n\n        public abstract long modulus();\n\n        public Modulus() {\n            factorial.add(1L);\n            invFactorial.add(1L);\n        }\n\n        public long factorial(int n) {\n            while (factorial.size() <= n) {\n                int size = factorial.size();\n                factorial.add(mult(factorial.get(size - 1), size));\n            }\n            return factorial.get(n);\n        }\n\n        public long invFactorial(int n) {\n            int last = invFactorial.size() - 1;\n            if (last < n) {\n                int needed = n - last;\n                long[] buffer = new long[needed];\n                buffer[0] = inv(factorial(n));\n                for (int i = 1; i < needed; i++) {\n                    buffer[i] = mult(buffer[i - 1], n - i + 1);\n                }\n                for (int i = needed - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n                    invFactorial.add(buffer[i]);\n                }\n            }\n            return invFactorial.get(n);\n        }\n\n        public long paritySign(long k) {\n            return (k % 2 == 0) ? 1 : modulus() - 1;\n        }\n\n        public long ncr(int n, int r) {\n            if (r < 0 || n < r) return 0;\n            return mult(factorial(n), mult(invFactorial(r), invFactorial(n - r)));\n        }\n\n        public long normalize(long x) {\n            x %= modulus();\n            return x < 0 ? x + modulus() : x;\n        }\n\n        public long add(long a, long b) {\n            long s = a + b;\n            return s < modulus() ? s : s - modulus();\n        }\n\n        public long subtract(long a, long b) {\n            long d = a - b;\n            return d < 0 ? d + modulus() : d;\n        }\n\n        public long mult(long... args) {\n      